WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:04.260
SPEAKER: When I was a sysadmin
back in pre-cloud days,

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.480
I had lots of little
scheduled shell scripts

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.930
on my workstation for admin
tasks like taking backups.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.060
But now that many
systems are in the cloud,

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.910
it makes sense to run our
admin scripts there too.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:17.460
Let's say we want to run this
Firestore database backup

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:18.630
every night in the cloud.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:22.350
First, we copy this simple Go
program that listens for HTTP

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.210
and invokes our shell script.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:27.090
Finally, we deploy the
container to Cloud Run.

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:29.670
If we hit the URL of our
new Cloud Run service,

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.710
our shell script
will run at once.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.620
If we set up Cloud
Scheduler to hit that URL,

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:37.140
the script can run unattended
in the middle of the night.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.750
It can report status
with the echo command.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.880
The output will go to
Cloud Logging, where we

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.450
can look at it in the morning.

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:46.170
We should also restrict access
to our Cloud Run service.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:47.950
See the link in the description.

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:49.590
Now we have a script
that runs nightly

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:52.050
without having to maintain
a server or virtual machine

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:53.500
for it.

